Why This Is Asked\n\nThis question probes understanding of practical sorting trade-offs beyond textbook asymptotics, including cache locality, in-place constraints, and robust pivot strategies. It invites discussion of 3-way partitioning to handle duplicates, median-of-three to curb worst-case behavior, and a small insertion sort for tiny partitions.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- 3-way partitioning in quicksort\n- median-of-three pivot selection\n- insertion sort threshold tuning\n- in-place algorithms and cache efficiency\n- iterative vs recursive implementations\n\n## Code Example\n```javascript
function sortInPlace(arr){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(a,i,j)=>{const t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  const medianOfThree=(a,i,j,k)=>{let x=[a[i],i],[a[j],j],[a[k],k].sort((p,q)=>p[0]-q[0]); return a[x[1]];};
  // iterative 3-way quicksort skeleton
  const stack=[[0,arr.length-1]];
  while(stack.length){
    const [lo,hi]=stack.pop();
    if(hi-lo+1<=TH){ // insertion sort for small partitions
      for(let i=lo+1;i<=hi;i++){
        let t=arr[i], j=i-1;
        while(j>=lo && arr[j]>t){ arr[j+1]=arr[j]; j-- }
        arr[j+1]=t;
      }
      continue;
    }
    // median-of-three pivot
    const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
    const pivot= medianOfThree(arr, lo, mid, hi);
    // 3-way partition
    let lt=lo, i=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(arr[i]<pivot) swap(arr, lt++, i++);
      else if(arr[i]>pivot) swap(arr, i, gt--);
      else i++;
    }
    stack.push([lo, lt-1]);
    stack.push([gt+1, hi]);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to model task dependencies as a DAG and compute an optimal parallel schedule using topological ordering and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological sort**: Ordering nodes such that all prerequisites come before dependent tasks
- **DAG DP**: Computing the longest path in a directed acyclic graph
- **Cycle detection**: Using indegree counts to identify cycles (if not all nodes are processed)
- **Time complexity**: O(n + m) where n is nodes and m is edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinish(n, edges, duration) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const finish = duration.slice();
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  }

  let processed = 0;

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + duration[v]);
      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }

  if (processed !== n) return -1;
  return Math.max(...finish);
}
```

## Key Takeaways

- Unlimited workers means tasks run in parallel when dependencies allow
- The finish time of a task is the maximum of (prerequisite finish + its own duration)
- The pipeline completes when the slowest path (critical path) finishes

## Why This Is Asked
